MARK MANDEL: Let's do that.

00:03:48.295 --> 00:03:49.690
So on this week's
podcast, we are

00:03:49.690 --> 00:03:52.690
excited to have join
us Bryan Catanzaro, who

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:55.505
is the VP of Applied Deep
Learning Research at NVIDIA.

00:03:55.505 --> 00:03:56.380
BRYAN CATANZARO: Hey.

00:03:56.380 --> 00:03:57.460
MELANIE WARRICK: Good afternoon.

00:03:57.460 --> 00:03:59.740
And so, Bryan, can you tell
us a little about yourself

00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:01.120
and the work that
you're doing now,

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:02.740
and the work you've
done in the past?

00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:03.656
BRYAN CATANZARO: Sure.

00:04:03.656 --> 00:04:05.950
So I lead a team of
researchers here at NVIDIA

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:08.830
that's focused on using
deep learning to make

00:04:08.830 --> 00:04:11.930
NVIDIA's products better and
NVIDIA's processes better.

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:14.080
And so we're looking at
a bunch of different ways

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:16.959
of using AI in lots
of different domains,

00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:21.040
ranging from video games to chip
design, and all sorts of stuff

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:21.800
in between.

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:24.794
So it's an exciting time to
be working in AI applications.

00:04:24.794 --> 00:04:26.710
There's a lot of diamonds
on the beach waiting

00:04:26.710 --> 00:04:28.570
for people to pick
them up and change

00:04:28.570 --> 00:04:29.720
the way that we do things.

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:31.870
And so it's a pretty
exciting project.

00:04:31.870 --> 00:04:33.036
MELANIE WARRICK: Definitely.

00:04:33.036 --> 00:04:36.130
And in terms of NVIDIA, I
know NVIDIA has lead the way

00:04:36.130 --> 00:04:37.750
when it comes to GPUs.

00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:40.600
And GPUs have played
such a significant role

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:43.259
in the deep learning space,
as well as the gaming space.

00:04:43.259 --> 00:04:44.800
We were talking
about this, actually,

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:46.299
right before we
started the podcast,

00:04:46.299 --> 00:04:49.780
but how impressive it is that
NVIDIA's strategy around GPUs

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:53.860
in particular has shifted
over the last few years alone.

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:56.380
And can you speak to
that a little bit?

00:04:56.380 --> 00:04:58.690
BRYAN CATANZARO: You know,
I think NVIDIA has always

00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:03.300
had a strong belief that
GPUs, as a throughput-oriented

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:08.080
processor, were going to
change the world beyond gaming.

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:10.060
And more than 10
years ago, NVIDIA

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:13.720
started investing in CUDA,
which is our language

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:15.490
and sort of ecosystem
for programming

00:05:15.490 --> 00:05:17.180
throughput-oriented computers.

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:20.360
And at the beginning, we were
a little bit less certain

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:23.780
about what applications were
going to need this power,

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:26.990
and what was actually going
to change in the world.

00:05:26.990 --> 00:05:29.870
Because we had this new way
of thinking about algorithms

00:05:29.870 --> 00:05:31.650
and computation.

00:05:31.650 --> 00:05:34.790
But I think the belief
has been consistent.

00:05:34.790 --> 00:05:37.550
And the focus has been
there for a long time.

00:05:37.550 --> 00:05:40.760
Deep learning happened
to be the most important

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:44.030
of all of the applications
that need high throughput

00:05:44.030 --> 00:05:44.810
computation.

00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:49.670
And so once NVIDIA saw that,
it was basically instant.

00:05:49.670 --> 00:05:51.650
The whole company
latched onto it,

00:05:51.650 --> 00:05:53.690
because they realized
this is our chance

00:05:53.690 --> 00:05:54.930
to make a difference.

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:57.764
And I happened to be involved
in that a little bit.

00:05:57.764 --> 00:05:59.180
I was one of the
very first people

00:05:59.180 --> 00:06:04.700
working on deep learning back
at NVIDIA Research back in 2012.

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:08.840
And we were having fun and
doing this research project.

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:11.570
And like many research
projects, it wasn't clear to me

00:06:11.570 --> 00:06:14.332
at the time whether this was
going to be something fun,

00:06:14.332 --> 00:06:16.040
or whether this was
going to be something

00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:17.780
that really changed the world.

00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:21.350
But the amount of interest in
our work was really surprising.

00:06:21.350 --> 00:06:23.330
The results were
good, and people

00:06:23.330 --> 00:06:25.010
started paying a lot
of attention to it.

00:06:25.010 --> 00:06:29.210
And I started meeting with a lot
of different people at NVIDIA.

00:06:29.210 --> 00:06:31.740
And they kind of saw--

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:33.930
the whole company kind
of saw-- like, wow,

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:36.260
this could be really big for us.

00:06:36.260 --> 00:06:37.990
And so everybody
latched onto it.

00:06:37.990 --> 00:06:39.320
MELANIE WARRICK: Do you
remember a specific time

00:06:39.320 --> 00:06:41.180
when you were doing that
research that stood out

00:06:41.180 --> 00:06:43.400
to you, that made you go,
oh, wow, this is much more

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:44.810
impactful than I expected?

00:06:44.810 --> 00:06:46.920
Or a specific moment
that comes to mind?

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:47.670
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:06:47.670 --> 00:06:49.850
So one of the things that
attracted some attention,

00:06:49.850 --> 00:06:53.080
we published a paper
at ICML in 2013.

00:06:53.080 --> 00:06:55.640
And this was me collaborating
with a group of researchers

00:06:55.640 --> 00:06:59.210
at Stanford, including
Adam Coates and Andrew Ng.

00:06:59.210 --> 00:07:02.120
We were able to take an approach
to unsupervised learning

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:04.520
that was pioneered, actually,
in the Google Brain team

00:07:04.520 --> 00:07:08.340
by Quoc Le, and we were able
to replicate that using far,

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:09.800
far fewer computers.

00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:12.230
So Quoc had published
this paper in 2012.

00:07:12.230 --> 00:07:16.040
And he needed something
like 1,000 nodes

00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:17.730
in order to train this model.

00:07:17.730 --> 00:07:20.570
And we were able to train the
model with three nodes using

00:07:20.570 --> 00:07:24.080
GPUs and more efficient software
that was kind of a little bit

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:25.350
more HPC-focused.

00:07:25.350 --> 00:07:27.240
And so we published
this paper at ICML.

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:29.420
And people were like, wow,
this is really exciting,

00:07:29.420 --> 00:07:31.970
because it
democratizes the work.

00:07:31.970 --> 00:07:34.340
If it makes deep
learning a thing

00:07:34.340 --> 00:07:36.494
that you can do on three
nodes instead of 1,000,

00:07:36.494 --> 00:07:37.910
then the number
of people that can

00:07:37.910 --> 00:07:39.920
start applying this
technology to new problems

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:41.880
just goes up dramatically.

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:44.450
And that, that was something
that caught a lot of attention.

00:07:44.450 --> 00:07:45.260
MELANIE WARRICK:
And just to confirm,

00:07:45.260 --> 00:07:46.760
when you're talking about
what you were working on,

00:07:46.760 --> 00:07:47.810
this is cuDNN.

00:07:47.810 --> 00:07:50.660
BRYAN CATANZARO: So this was
actually before cuDNN existed.

00:07:50.660 --> 00:07:52.662
So I did this with
Adam and Andrew.

00:07:52.662 --> 00:07:53.870
And that was really exciting.

00:07:53.870 --> 00:07:56.390
And I started talking to more
deep learning researchers.

00:07:56.390 --> 00:08:00.020
And actually, the person that
inspired me to make cuDNN

00:08:00.020 --> 00:08:03.260
was Rob Fergus, who was
a professor at New York

00:08:03.260 --> 00:08:05.780
University, one of Yann
LeCun's collaborators.

00:08:05.780 --> 00:08:08.000
And I think Rob is
now at Facebook.

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:10.247
And Rob was like,
look, we are doing

00:08:10.247 --> 00:08:12.080
all these convolutional
models all the time.

00:08:12.080 --> 00:08:14.300
And we've written
our own convolutions,

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:16.610
but we think it's a lot
of work to make them fast.

00:08:16.610 --> 00:08:20.690
And NVIDIA GPUs change all
the time, because that's

00:08:20.690 --> 00:08:22.940
the primary way
NVIDIA innovates,

00:08:22.940 --> 00:08:25.149
is by making the
architecture better.

00:08:25.149 --> 00:08:26.690
But then that means
that the software

00:08:26.690 --> 00:08:28.480
needs to adapt to
the new architecture.

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:30.080
And so, Rob was
telling me, we're

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:32.090
spending too much time
working on these kernels.

00:08:32.090 --> 00:08:34.340
We think NVIDIA should
make a library for it.

00:08:34.340 --> 00:08:36.440
And I thought, yeah,
that's a great idea.

00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:39.289
So then I set about
convincing the rest of NVIDIA

00:08:39.289 --> 00:08:41.010
that they should
make this product.

00:08:41.010 --> 00:08:42.390
And it took a
little bit of work.

00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:44.900
So while I was
convincing them, I just

00:08:44.900 --> 00:08:48.120
started writing the code
for a research prototype.

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:49.880
And then as the
conversations progressed,

00:08:49.880 --> 00:08:50.970
things got more concrete.

00:08:50.970 --> 00:08:53.210
And then finally, people
kind of caught the vision

00:08:53.210 --> 00:08:55.280
and decided, yes, we
should productize this.

00:08:55.280 --> 00:08:57.230
And that became cuDNN.

00:08:57.230 --> 00:08:59.010
MARK MANDEL: So this
sounds really cool.

00:08:59.010 --> 00:09:01.040
I know, as we've said
before on the podcast,

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:02.210
Melanie's the MLs expert.

00:09:02.210 --> 00:09:03.790
And I'm sort of the person--

00:09:03.790 --> 00:09:04.790
MELANIE WARRICK: You're
the gaming expert.

00:09:04.790 --> 00:09:06.860
MARK MANDEL: --who
barely understands math.

00:09:06.860 --> 00:09:10.489
For those people who aren't as
familiar with this ecosystem,

00:09:10.489 --> 00:09:12.280
and maybe you are more
like, doesn't NVIDIA

00:09:12.280 --> 00:09:13.800
make gaming cards, first of--

00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:14.758
BRYAN CATANZARO: We do.

00:09:14.758 --> 00:09:17.740
MARK MANDEL: But how does
that translate to doing--

00:09:17.740 --> 00:09:19.952
what is that pathway from,
I have a graphics card.

00:09:19.952 --> 00:09:21.410
I thought it was
just for graphics.

00:09:21.410 --> 00:09:24.020
Why is it also applicable,
or how is it also applicable,

00:09:24.020 --> 00:09:26.000
to doing what seems to
be intense mathematical

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:26.720
calculations?

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:30.380
BRYAN CATANZARO: When you
draw a picture in a 3D game,

00:09:30.380 --> 00:09:33.350
you're essentially running a
lot of programs that figure out

00:09:33.350 --> 00:09:36.410
what every pixel looks like.

00:09:36.410 --> 00:09:39.020
And over the years,
those programs

00:09:39.020 --> 00:09:41.690
have gotten more complex,
as you might expect,

00:09:41.690 --> 00:09:43.890
and also a lot more flexible.

00:09:43.890 --> 00:09:47.210
It used to be that GPUs were
very fixed-function machines,

00:09:47.210 --> 00:09:49.320
where you kind of just
gave it a list of triangles

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:51.080
and it rasterized them.

00:09:51.080 --> 00:09:55.070
But nowadays, to do the
complex lighting and texturing

00:09:55.070 --> 00:09:57.590
and reflections, and
so forth, all that

00:09:57.590 --> 00:10:01.040
is done by writing programs,
but very parallel programs.

00:10:01.040 --> 00:10:03.260
And so it turns out,
actually, that the workload

00:10:03.260 --> 00:10:05.990
in modern games often is--

00:10:05.990 --> 00:10:10.340
majority of the time is being
spent running compute programs

00:10:10.340 --> 00:10:13.424
rather than running
traditional graphics workloads.

00:10:13.424 --> 00:10:14.840
And so there has
been an evolution

00:10:14.840 --> 00:10:17.450
in graphics towards more
flexibility and more

00:10:17.450 --> 00:10:20.850
parallel computing, and less
fixed-function graphics.

00:10:20.850 --> 00:10:24.170
And so GPUs have had to
evolve to support that.

00:10:24.170 --> 00:10:28.862
At the same time, if you have a
really high throughput parallel

00:10:28.862 --> 00:10:31.070
processor that can be used
for arbitrary computation,

00:10:31.070 --> 00:10:33.630
it turns out there's a lot of
things that you can do with it,

00:10:33.630 --> 00:10:34.820
including AI.

00:10:34.820 --> 00:10:36.630
MELANIE WARRICK:
Well, and I'm curious,

00:10:36.630 --> 00:10:39.950
how did you even get involved
with parallel processing

00:10:39.950 --> 00:10:41.000
compute?

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:43.252
What inspired you to go down
this path to begin with?

00:10:43.252 --> 00:10:44.960
BRYAN CATANZARO: When
I was an undergrad,

00:10:44.960 --> 00:10:46.610
I did some internships at Intel.

00:10:46.610 --> 00:10:48.170
And I was working
on circuit design.

00:10:48.170 --> 00:10:50.630
And I made some really,
really fast circuits

00:10:50.630 --> 00:10:53.140
that were going to run
at like 10 gigahertz.

00:10:53.140 --> 00:10:55.490
And this was for
transistors that hadn't even

00:10:55.490 --> 00:10:56.120
been built yet.

00:10:56.120 --> 00:10:58.010
So we were kind of
dreaming about the future

00:10:58.010 --> 00:10:59.600
and trying to build it.

00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:02.780
And we kept figuring out that
the power consumption was

00:11:02.780 --> 00:11:04.724
going to be impossible.

00:11:04.724 --> 00:11:06.140
There was just no
way that we were

00:11:06.140 --> 00:11:09.860
going to be able to make a
single-threaded computer that

00:11:09.860 --> 00:11:11.870
held onto the performance
scaling trends

00:11:11.870 --> 00:11:13.370
that everyone expected.

00:11:13.370 --> 00:11:17.000
So I saw at Intel
this realization

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:19.670
that the world of
computing and the future

00:11:19.670 --> 00:11:22.340
must become parallel.

00:11:22.340 --> 00:11:24.260
And so when I went
to graduate school,

00:11:24.260 --> 00:11:27.590
I decided to focus on
parallel computing, frameworks

00:11:27.590 --> 00:11:29.060
for programming
parallel computers,

00:11:29.060 --> 00:11:31.160
and applications of
parallel computers.

00:11:31.160 --> 00:11:34.460
And the one that really
struck me as most promising

00:11:34.460 --> 00:11:35.540
was machine learning.

00:11:35.540 --> 00:11:39.980
And so, that's how I ended up
spending my PhD on programming

00:11:39.980 --> 00:11:43.550
frameworks for parallel
processors and machine learning

00:11:43.550 --> 00:11:44.150
applications.

00:11:44.150 --> 00:11:45.890
Because I felt like that
was the most important thing

00:11:45.890 --> 00:11:47.670
you can do with a
parallel computer.

00:11:47.670 --> 00:11:47.990
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:11:47.990 --> 00:11:50.577
And then I know, in terms of
your background, you were here,

00:11:50.577 --> 00:11:51.660
you were working on cuDNN.

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:53.932
And then you made a shift,
and you joined Baidu.

00:11:53.932 --> 00:11:54.890
BRYAN CATANZARO: I did.

00:11:54.890 --> 00:11:57.110
MELANIE WARRICK: And you were
on machine learning with Baidu.

00:11:57.110 --> 00:11:58.250
And then you've come back.

00:11:58.250 --> 00:11:58.580
BRYAN CATANZARO: That's right.

00:11:58.580 --> 00:12:00.410
MELANIE WARRICK: What's
led to that trajectory?

00:12:00.410 --> 00:12:01.993
BRYAN CATANZARO:
Well, so, I mentioned

00:12:01.993 --> 00:12:05.180
before that my introduction
to the world of deep learning

00:12:05.180 --> 00:12:08.300
happened with Andrew
Ng and Adam Coates.

00:12:08.300 --> 00:12:12.170
They founded this lab at Baidu,
the Silicon Valley AI lab,

00:12:12.170 --> 00:12:13.500
and asked me to come join them.

00:12:13.500 --> 00:12:15.800
And I felt like it was a
great opportunity for me

00:12:15.800 --> 00:12:18.350
to get closer to applications.

00:12:18.350 --> 00:12:20.390
I really loved working on cuDNN.

00:12:20.390 --> 00:12:22.040
I'm very proud of it.

00:12:22.040 --> 00:12:25.640
And I felt like I wanted to
be a little bit closer to how

00:12:25.640 --> 00:12:30.349
we take AI and make it into
something that solves a problem

00:12:30.349 --> 00:12:31.890
that people really
need to be solved.

00:12:31.890 --> 00:12:35.210
And so I felt like working
with the Silicon Valley AI

00:12:35.210 --> 00:12:36.920
lab at Baidu gave
me a great chance

00:12:36.920 --> 00:12:39.660
to get closer to applications.

00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:42.980
And then I'd been there
for about 2 and 1/2 years,

00:12:42.980 --> 00:12:44.600
and I felt like I
had accomplished

00:12:44.600 --> 00:12:46.670
my mission, was looking
around to figure out

00:12:46.670 --> 00:12:48.350
what my next project should be.

00:12:48.350 --> 00:12:51.810
And Jensen Huang, NVIDIA'S CEO,
called me up and said, hey,

00:12:51.810 --> 00:12:54.110
we'd really like you
to come back to NVIDIA

00:12:54.110 --> 00:12:57.260
and found a lab focused
on research figuring out

00:12:57.260 --> 00:12:59.864
how to apply AI to problems
that we have at NVIDIA.

00:12:59.864 --> 00:13:01.280
And I thought,
well, that's great.

00:13:01.280 --> 00:13:02.780
Because I love NVIDIA.

00:13:02.780 --> 00:13:05.510
I've always been sort
of an ally of NVIDIA.

00:13:05.510 --> 00:13:07.550
Because I believe
in the technology.

00:13:07.550 --> 00:13:11.049
And a chance to come back
and focus on the applications

00:13:11.049 --> 00:13:12.590
that I really wanted
to do, it seemed

00:13:12.590 --> 00:13:13.632
like a great opportunity.

00:13:13.632 --> 00:13:16.006
MELANIE WARRICK: What kind of
application do you work on?

00:13:16.006 --> 00:13:18.200
BRYAN CATANZARO: So my
team right now is mostly

00:13:18.200 --> 00:13:20.070
focused on three areas.

00:13:20.070 --> 00:13:22.400
One of them is graphics.

00:13:22.400 --> 00:13:23.840
One of them is text.

00:13:23.840 --> 00:13:24.980
And the other is audio.

00:13:24.980 --> 00:13:25.500
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:26.916
BRYAN CATANZARO:
So with graphics,

00:13:26.916 --> 00:13:29.150
we're really excited
about the opportunities

00:13:29.150 --> 00:13:31.940
to change the way that graphics
is created and rendered

00:13:31.940 --> 00:13:33.840
by using deep learning.

00:13:33.840 --> 00:13:36.500
We have a paper, actually,
in this year's CVPR

00:13:36.500 --> 00:13:40.530
that is about a generative
model for creating graphics--

00:13:40.530 --> 00:13:43.370
photo-realistic,
high-resolution graphics--

00:13:43.370 --> 00:13:46.740
given very simplistic
input images.

00:13:46.740 --> 00:13:48.830
And so the idea would
be that we could create

00:13:48.830 --> 00:13:52.310
a new kind of graphics engine,
where the traditional graphics

00:13:52.310 --> 00:13:54.610
is actually doing very
little work at all,

00:13:54.610 --> 00:13:56.360
and the majority of
the graphics rendering

00:13:56.360 --> 00:13:58.520
is being done by a
generative model.

00:13:58.520 --> 00:14:00.860
And the advantage
of doing that would

00:14:00.860 --> 00:14:04.070
be that we believe it will
be far cheaper to create

00:14:04.070 --> 00:14:04.970
virtual worlds.

00:14:04.970 --> 00:14:07.710
Because in order to
create a new world,

00:14:07.710 --> 00:14:09.620
we just need to
train a new model

00:14:09.620 --> 00:14:13.640
rather than spend a lot of money
with an army of artists that

00:14:13.640 --> 00:14:15.860
can manually create
all the textures

00:14:15.860 --> 00:14:18.180
and lighting that go
into that environment.

00:14:18.180 --> 00:14:19.680
So we're pretty
excited about that.

00:14:19.680 --> 00:14:22.520
And we feel like there
is a lot of applications

00:14:22.520 --> 00:14:25.172
for AI in graphics.

00:14:25.172 --> 00:14:27.380
And so that's one of the
things going on in my group.

00:14:27.380 --> 00:14:30.170
We're also working on
some text problems.

00:14:30.170 --> 00:14:32.540
We're really inspired
by this OpenAI paper,

00:14:32.540 --> 00:14:35.420
doing unsupervised learning
for text classifications,

00:14:35.420 --> 00:14:38.120
specifically sentiment
analysis, where

00:14:38.120 --> 00:14:41.270
you train a model to reconstruct
a large text database

00:14:41.270 --> 00:14:42.230
character by character.

00:14:42.230 --> 00:14:44.210
And it turns out
that in so doing,

00:14:44.210 --> 00:14:47.210
your model has to understand
the meaning of the text,

00:14:47.210 --> 00:14:49.250
not just the characters
and the text.

00:14:49.250 --> 00:14:53.450
And you can use that with
just a little bit of labels

00:14:53.450 --> 00:14:56.420
to solve some really
applied problems,

00:14:56.420 --> 00:14:57.780
like sentiment analysis.

00:14:57.780 --> 00:15:00.420
And so we're working
on stuff like that.

00:15:00.420 --> 00:15:02.510
And then in the audio
space, right now

00:15:02.510 --> 00:15:05.510
we're working on some things
related to speech and speech

00:15:05.510 --> 00:15:07.100
synthesis.

00:15:07.100 --> 00:15:09.650
MELANIE WARRICK: So in terms
of doing this type of work,

00:15:09.650 --> 00:15:12.635
you're trying to also set it up
to make it accessible to users

00:15:12.635 --> 00:15:14.184
of the GPUs, I assume.

00:15:14.184 --> 00:15:15.350
BRYAN CATANZARO: Absolutely.

00:15:15.350 --> 00:15:15.849
Yeah.

00:15:15.849 --> 00:15:17.930
One of the great things
about working at NVIDIA

00:15:17.930 --> 00:15:20.810
is that we get to open
source a lot of the work

00:15:20.810 --> 00:15:21.689
that we're doing.

00:15:21.689 --> 00:15:23.730
In fact, all of the projects
that I'm mentioning,

00:15:23.730 --> 00:15:25.230
we have either open
sourced or we're

00:15:25.230 --> 00:15:26.660
in the process of open sourcing.

00:15:26.660 --> 00:15:28.940
And one of the reasons
that we can do that

00:15:28.940 --> 00:15:33.590
is because the more we
inspire other people to do AI,

00:15:33.590 --> 00:15:35.900
then the better
it is for NVIDIA.

00:15:35.900 --> 00:15:38.810
Because so much of
AI happens on GPUs.

00:15:38.810 --> 00:15:41.180
And so we can make the
world a better place

00:15:41.180 --> 00:15:43.340
by giving people new
technology that they

00:15:43.340 --> 00:15:44.510
can use to solve problems.

00:15:44.510 --> 00:15:46.340
And it also helps
NVIDIA's business.

00:15:46.340 --> 00:15:49.702
And so it's a great thing for
us to open source our work.

00:15:49.702 --> 00:15:50.660
MELANIE WARRICK: Right.

00:15:50.660 --> 00:15:53.270
And then, I know you've done
the work in the past on cuDNN,

00:15:53.270 --> 00:15:55.910
and really driving
software to help people

00:15:55.910 --> 00:15:58.550
optimize working on GPUs.

00:15:58.550 --> 00:16:00.710
But then there's GPUs
that have been actually

00:16:00.710 --> 00:16:03.896
built, specifically Volta,
to help with deep learning.

00:16:03.896 --> 00:16:05.270
Can you speak to
you a little bit

00:16:05.270 --> 00:16:08.030
about Volta, and how
it works and helps

00:16:08.030 --> 00:16:10.654
people improve their
processing of deep learning?

00:16:10.654 --> 00:16:11.570
BRYAN CATANZARO: Yeah.

00:16:11.570 --> 00:16:15.320
So, Volta is the
first GPU that NVIDIA

00:16:15.320 --> 00:16:20.720
made after it woke up to the
possibilities of deep learning.

00:16:20.720 --> 00:16:24.220
And NVIDIA believes,
I think more strongly

00:16:24.220 --> 00:16:26.090
than any other organization
that I've seen,

00:16:26.090 --> 00:16:28.640
in the power of
specialized architecture.

00:16:28.640 --> 00:16:30.980
That's really what
has made NVIDIA

00:16:30.980 --> 00:16:34.460
successful from the beginning,
is the company builds chips

00:16:34.460 --> 00:16:37.820
to solve important problems,
and that are tailored

00:16:37.820 --> 00:16:39.690
to those particular problems.

00:16:39.690 --> 00:16:41.780
So when NVIDIA
decided, deep learning

00:16:41.780 --> 00:16:43.880
is the most important
thing that we're

00:16:43.880 --> 00:16:46.394
going to be using our
GPUs for, the next step

00:16:46.394 --> 00:16:48.560
was to customize the
architecture for deep learning.

00:16:48.560 --> 00:16:51.140
And Volta is the first
fruits of that effort.

00:16:51.140 --> 00:16:54.030
And so, the computational
capabilities of Volta

00:16:54.030 --> 00:16:55.670
are really astounding.

00:16:55.670 --> 00:16:59.960
One Volta GPU can run
at over 120 teraflops

00:16:59.960 --> 00:17:01.340
when you're training a model.

00:17:01.340 --> 00:17:07.220
And that's about 10 times
faster than the speed

00:17:07.220 --> 00:17:10.040
that you would get on
our prior generation GPU.

00:17:10.040 --> 00:17:13.707
And so that's a really
exciting capability.

00:17:13.707 --> 00:17:15.290
MARK MANDEL: How
does the architecture

00:17:15.290 --> 00:17:17.873
for something like Volta differ
from something like a graphics

00:17:17.873 --> 00:17:18.440
GPU?

00:17:18.440 --> 00:17:19.760
BRYAN CATANZARO: Volta
is different in a lot

00:17:19.760 --> 00:17:21.440
of ways from our previous GPUs.

00:17:21.440 --> 00:17:24.060
It's just a lot more focused
on compute workloads.

00:17:24.060 --> 00:17:25.819
So especially the
memory subsystem

00:17:25.819 --> 00:17:29.810
has been beefed up to handle
the kinds of data patterns

00:17:29.810 --> 00:17:33.110
that are required in a
lot of compute workloads.

00:17:33.110 --> 00:17:36.180
It also has a lot faster
integer arithmetic,

00:17:36.180 --> 00:17:39.260
which is really useful for
addressing math and more,

00:17:39.260 --> 00:17:41.180
I would say,
complicated workloads

00:17:41.180 --> 00:17:42.980
where you need to do
a lot of indexing math

00:17:42.980 --> 00:17:46.100
to figure out what data you're
going to load and operate on.

00:17:46.100 --> 00:17:49.040
So the whole architecture
has been revamped.

00:17:49.040 --> 00:17:51.470
And then, in addition to
all those other features,

00:17:51.470 --> 00:17:55.310
we added Tensor Cores,
which are essentially

00:17:55.310 --> 00:17:59.660
matrix multiply units that
can do small matrix, 4 by 4

00:17:59.660 --> 00:18:01.940
by 4, matrix multiplications.

00:18:01.940 --> 00:18:05.480
And the thing that's really
great about Tensor Cores

00:18:05.480 --> 00:18:08.570
is that they dramatically
reduce the energy

00:18:08.570 --> 00:18:13.291
cost of reading and writing
the operands from the register

00:18:13.291 --> 00:18:13.790
file.

00:18:13.790 --> 00:18:17.370
Because the majority of the work
happens inside of this bigger,

00:18:17.370 --> 00:18:19.520
chunkier unit, and you
don't have to be constantly

00:18:19.520 --> 00:18:21.710
reading and writing all of
the intermediate results

00:18:21.710 --> 00:18:23.020
to register file.

00:18:23.020 --> 00:18:25.190
And so that dramatically
reduces the energy

00:18:25.190 --> 00:18:27.950
that's required to do a
matrix multiplication, which

00:18:27.950 --> 00:18:31.124
then makes the processor
dramatically more efficient.

00:18:31.124 --> 00:18:32.040
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:18:32.040 --> 00:18:34.400
So, you mentioned
about teraflops.

00:18:34.400 --> 00:18:37.520
Can you help-- for those who
might not understand teraflops,

00:18:37.520 --> 00:18:40.280
exaflops-- explain a
little bit more, I guess,

00:18:40.280 --> 00:18:41.750
in layman's terms
what that means,

00:18:41.750 --> 00:18:43.333
and what they can
really conceptualize

00:18:43.333 --> 00:18:44.120
in terms of speed?

00:18:44.120 --> 00:18:44.660
BRYAN CATANZARO: Yeah.

00:18:44.660 --> 00:18:46.374
Well, it's hard,
actually, for humans

00:18:46.374 --> 00:18:47.540
to understand these numbers.

00:18:47.540 --> 00:18:48.998
Because they're
really big numbers.

00:18:48.998 --> 00:18:50.930
But tera is 10 to 12th.

00:18:50.930 --> 00:18:57.800
So 120 teraflops, that's
120 trillion math operations

00:18:57.800 --> 00:18:59.164
per second, which is a lot.

00:18:59.164 --> 00:19:00.080
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah.

00:19:00.080 --> 00:19:01.130
That is a lot.

00:19:01.130 --> 00:19:03.380
BRYAN CATANZARO: And training
a deep learning model

00:19:03.380 --> 00:19:04.960
can often require exaflops.

00:19:04.960 --> 00:19:06.697
So, after tera comes peta.

00:19:06.697 --> 00:19:07.780
So, that's 10 to the 15th.

00:19:07.780 --> 00:19:11.120
And after peta comes
exa, which is 10 to 18th.

00:19:11.120 --> 00:19:13.700
And so training a
deep learning model

00:19:13.700 --> 00:19:17.040
routinely takes
tens of exaflops.

00:19:17.040 --> 00:19:19.100
I like to point out
that people often

00:19:19.100 --> 00:19:22.130
estimate the number of grains
of sand on planet Earth

00:19:22.130 --> 00:19:25.340
at about 10 exagrains of sand.

00:19:25.340 --> 00:19:28.700
So if you're doing 10 exaflops,
it's kind of like a multiply

00:19:28.700 --> 00:19:32.705
or an add for every
single grain of sand.

00:19:32.705 --> 00:19:35.060
And you do that every
time you train a model.

00:19:35.060 --> 00:19:36.914
So it's extremely
computationally intense.

00:19:36.914 --> 00:19:38.580
MELANIE WARRICK: And
it turns it around.

00:19:38.580 --> 00:19:39.890
I mean, the speed at
which it can turn around

00:19:39.890 --> 00:19:42.050
some of these models that are
becoming larger and larger,

00:19:42.050 --> 00:19:44.470
and have more data that
they're trying to process, is--

00:19:44.470 --> 00:19:45.690
it has its impact.

00:19:45.690 --> 00:19:47.440
It's very important
at the end of the day.

00:19:47.440 --> 00:19:48.170
BRYAN CATANZARO: It is.

00:19:48.170 --> 00:19:48.670
Yeah.

00:19:48.670 --> 00:19:51.740
I mean, when you're trying
to solve a problem with AI,

00:19:51.740 --> 00:19:52.910
you have to iterate.

00:19:52.910 --> 00:19:55.280
Because it's a search.

00:19:55.280 --> 00:19:56.810
You have to find
the right model.

00:19:56.810 --> 00:19:58.601
You have to find the
right hyperparameters.

00:19:58.601 --> 00:19:59.990
You have to find the right data.

00:19:59.990 --> 00:20:01.760
And that requires
a lot of search.

00:20:01.760 --> 00:20:05.777
And so, as you create a
model, and you train it,

00:20:05.777 --> 00:20:06.860
you learn things about it.

00:20:06.860 --> 00:20:08.252
You learn when it is doing well.

00:20:08.252 --> 00:20:09.710
You learn when it's
not doing well.

00:20:09.710 --> 00:20:10.520
You find bugs.

00:20:10.520 --> 00:20:12.920
You fix hyperparameters.

00:20:12.920 --> 00:20:15.530
This requires training the
model many, many times.

00:20:15.530 --> 00:20:18.770
And the quicker you can do
it, the faster a developer

00:20:18.770 --> 00:20:21.980
can iterate, and that
leads to better results.

00:20:21.980 --> 00:20:24.440
And so people find
that having access

00:20:24.440 --> 00:20:27.050
to this kind of
compute capability,

00:20:27.050 --> 00:20:28.907
it not just makes
the work go faster,

00:20:28.907 --> 00:20:30.740
but it actually changes
the kind of problems

00:20:30.740 --> 00:20:32.000
that they can attempt.

00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:34.030
Because there's problems
that they can try that

00:20:34.030 --> 00:20:36.470
they just never would have
been able to try without it.

00:20:36.470 --> 00:20:37.880
MELANIE WARRICK: And when
you talk about training,

00:20:37.880 --> 00:20:39.950
there's training, and
there's also inference.

00:20:39.950 --> 00:20:44.210
And so Volta is a wonderful
GPU to be using from a training

00:20:44.210 --> 00:20:44.720
perspective.

00:20:44.720 --> 00:20:47.240
Is that also architected
for inference?

00:20:47.240 --> 00:20:49.600
Or do you have other
options specifically?

00:20:49.600 --> 00:20:50.660
And I know the answer.

00:20:50.660 --> 00:20:52.670
But I wanted to give
you the chance to--

00:20:52.670 --> 00:20:52.860
BRYAN CATANZARO: Yeah.

00:20:52.860 --> 00:20:54.860
So let me explain a little
bit of the difference

00:20:54.860 --> 00:20:56.450
between training
and inference first.

00:20:56.450 --> 00:20:57.320
MELANIE WARRICK:
That would be great.

00:20:57.320 --> 00:20:59.630
BRYAN CATANZARO: Training
is a search, like I said.

00:20:59.630 --> 00:21:01.400
You're trying to find a model.

00:21:01.400 --> 00:21:04.070
So the model may have hundreds
of millions or billions

00:21:04.070 --> 00:21:05.000
of parameters.

00:21:05.000 --> 00:21:07.190
And you need to fine
tune every one of those

00:21:07.190 --> 00:21:08.850
to find the right number.

00:21:08.850 --> 00:21:10.490
And so it's a search.

00:21:10.490 --> 00:21:13.430
And you're searching for
it guided by your data.

00:21:13.430 --> 00:21:15.684
And so we often talk
about back propagation.

00:21:15.684 --> 00:21:17.600
What that means is you
do forward propagation,

00:21:17.600 --> 00:21:20.120
where you use the model
to make a guess about what

00:21:20.120 --> 00:21:22.790
the output should be-- say, if
we're doing speech recognition,

00:21:22.790 --> 00:21:26.060
and the input is a wave
file, and the output is text.

00:21:26.060 --> 00:21:27.800
So we run a bunch of
WAV files through,

00:21:27.800 --> 00:21:30.860
and we get the guesses for
what those WAV files contain.

00:21:30.860 --> 00:21:34.300
But then we know what the
right answer should be.

00:21:34.300 --> 00:21:35.630
We have what's called labels.

00:21:35.630 --> 00:21:39.620
We can use those labels to
figure out what the error was,

00:21:39.620 --> 00:21:41.060
where was the model wrong.

00:21:41.060 --> 00:21:42.920
And then we use that
to update the weights,

00:21:42.920 --> 00:21:45.260
just tweak them,
just a little bit.

00:21:45.260 --> 00:21:48.260
And we do that a million
times, or a billion times.

00:21:48.260 --> 00:21:51.230
And so through this process,
it's kind of evolutionarily.

00:21:51.230 --> 00:21:54.540
We search through this
space to find a good model.

00:21:54.540 --> 00:21:56.280
So that's the training process.

00:21:56.280 --> 00:21:59.750
The serving process, or
inference process, that's

00:21:59.750 --> 00:22:02.090
just performing the
forward propagation.

00:22:02.090 --> 00:22:05.356
Or given an input, what is
the output of that model.

00:22:05.356 --> 00:22:07.730
So we're just pushing the data
forward through the model.

00:22:07.730 --> 00:22:08.600
MELANIE WARRICK:
Just the prediction,

00:22:08.600 --> 00:22:09.080
or the classification.

00:22:09.080 --> 00:22:09.515
BRYAN CATANZARO:
Just the prediction,

00:22:09.515 --> 00:22:11.250
or the classification.

00:22:11.250 --> 00:22:13.130
So that computationally
is simpler,

00:22:13.130 --> 00:22:15.440
because it's only
doing the forward pass.

00:22:15.440 --> 00:22:16.880
It's not doing
the backward pass.

00:22:16.880 --> 00:22:18.870
And not doing the learning.

00:22:18.870 --> 00:22:21.710
But it has different
characteristics,

00:22:21.710 --> 00:22:22.980
algorithmically.

00:22:22.980 --> 00:22:27.170
And so it makes sense to have
software and hardware that

00:22:27.170 --> 00:22:30.060
is tailored for both of
them in different ways.

00:22:30.060 --> 00:22:32.690
And so it turns out
Volta is actually

00:22:32.690 --> 00:22:35.060
a great GPU for inference.

00:22:35.060 --> 00:22:37.550
I'm actually very excited about
using Voltas for inference.

00:22:37.550 --> 00:22:40.130
The biggest problem, I
think, right now with Voltas

00:22:40.130 --> 00:22:42.690
is that there's just not
enough Voltas in the world.

00:22:42.690 --> 00:22:44.960
And all the Voltas
that we make get

00:22:44.960 --> 00:22:47.870
sucked up to go into training,
because people need that

00:22:47.870 --> 00:22:48.830
so much.

00:22:48.830 --> 00:22:53.150
Whereas, inference
workloads are often smaller,

00:22:53.150 --> 00:22:56.339
and so people can get by
with smaller processors.

00:22:56.339 --> 00:22:58.130
But I do believe, in
the future, that we're

00:22:58.130 --> 00:23:00.260
going to see big
inference workloads that

00:23:00.260 --> 00:23:02.940
require even a Volta to deploy.

00:23:02.940 --> 00:23:05.560
So I am excited about both
training and inference

00:23:05.560 --> 00:23:06.060
for Volta.

00:23:06.060 --> 00:23:10.220
But NVIDIA also has other
products specifically designed

00:23:10.220 --> 00:23:11.360
for the inference market.

00:23:11.360 --> 00:23:14.690
For example, the Tesla
P4, it's a small--

00:23:14.690 --> 00:23:16.570
I think it's 50 to 75-watt--

00:23:16.570 --> 00:23:17.210
GPU.

00:23:17.210 --> 00:23:21.440
And it has some low-precision
arithmetic instructions.

00:23:21.440 --> 00:23:25.370
Because in inference, you
don't need nearly as many bits

00:23:25.370 --> 00:23:28.160
in order to get a good result
as you do during training.

00:23:28.160 --> 00:23:32.780
And so the Tesla P4
is a really good ops

00:23:32.780 --> 00:23:35.789
per second per watt processor
for the inference market.

00:23:35.789 --> 00:23:37.580
MARK MANDEL: You said
something interesting

00:23:37.580 --> 00:23:39.800
just before, where you were
talking about with the newer

00:23:39.800 --> 00:23:41.360
GPUs, you were able
to do things you

00:23:41.360 --> 00:23:43.640
weren't possibly able
to do before, possibly

00:23:43.640 --> 00:23:44.360
inspirationally.

00:23:44.360 --> 00:23:46.984
What are the things that you're
able to do now that you weren't

00:23:46.984 --> 00:23:48.580
actually able to do before?

00:23:48.580 --> 00:23:51.230
BRYAN CATANZARO: Well, I'll
speak a little bit to graphics,

00:23:51.230 --> 00:23:53.840
because we're really excited
about that at NVIDIA.

00:23:53.840 --> 00:23:58.820
But recently, there's been some
incredible generative models

00:23:58.820 --> 00:24:02.390
for graphics that we've seen,
including the progressive GAN,

00:24:02.390 --> 00:24:04.190
which generates very
high-resolution,

00:24:04.190 --> 00:24:06.980
very high-quality images.

00:24:06.980 --> 00:24:10.310
They trained it on the
celeb face data set.

00:24:10.310 --> 00:24:12.740
And the faces that
it generates are

00:24:12.740 --> 00:24:16.490
pretty indistinguishable
from real pictures of people,

00:24:16.490 --> 00:24:19.740
even though they're completely
invented by this model.

00:24:19.740 --> 00:24:24.560
And I think the leap
in fidelity for GANs,

00:24:24.560 --> 00:24:27.800
when you are able to train them
on larger data sets for longer

00:24:27.800 --> 00:24:30.230
periods of time,
is quite incredible

00:24:30.230 --> 00:24:32.180
and changes the
kinds of applications

00:24:32.180 --> 00:24:33.680
you can do with GANs.

00:24:33.680 --> 00:24:36.500
The paper that I mentioned
that we've been working on

00:24:36.500 --> 00:24:38.960
about synthesizing
photorealistic images

00:24:38.960 --> 00:24:43.070
from semantic labels,
that work also, I think,

00:24:43.070 --> 00:24:44.150
is pretty surprising.

00:24:44.150 --> 00:24:45.890
I often look at the
output of that model,

00:24:45.890 --> 00:24:48.230
and it takes me quite
a long time to figure

00:24:48.230 --> 00:24:51.990
out is this a fake image
or is this a real image.

00:24:51.990 --> 00:24:53.750
And that just would
not have been possible

00:24:53.750 --> 00:24:56.017
without this processing power.

00:24:56.017 --> 00:24:57.600
MELANIE WARRICK:
When I first met you,

00:24:57.600 --> 00:25:02.480
you were doing a presentation
on parallelizing data for models

00:25:02.480 --> 00:25:04.070
versus paralyzing a model.

00:25:04.070 --> 00:25:06.230
And there's different
methodologies

00:25:06.230 --> 00:25:08.840
in terms of how
do you split out--

00:25:08.840 --> 00:25:10.910
especially if
you're trying to do

00:25:10.910 --> 00:25:13.100
mass processing of a
deep learning model

00:25:13.100 --> 00:25:14.930
in particular-- how
do you break that out

00:25:14.930 --> 00:25:17.211
if you wanted to try to
do distributed computing.

00:25:17.211 --> 00:25:19.710
What are your thoughts now in
terms of distributed computing

00:25:19.710 --> 00:25:20.640
for deep learning?

00:25:20.640 --> 00:25:23.520
Do you see that is
really a thing to do?

00:25:23.520 --> 00:25:26.630
Or do you see just making
these chips better?

00:25:26.630 --> 00:25:27.880
BRYAN CATANZARO: We need both.

00:25:27.880 --> 00:25:30.750
So the great thing about
making the chip better

00:25:30.750 --> 00:25:34.380
is that it plugs into
the software environment

00:25:34.380 --> 00:25:36.315
without disruption.

00:25:36.315 --> 00:25:38.080
Or the disruption is much less.

00:25:38.080 --> 00:25:40.650
And so that allows
people to smoothly move

00:25:40.650 --> 00:25:43.260
from one generation to the
next and get performance

00:25:43.260 --> 00:25:46.980
improvements without having to
do big changes to their code.

00:25:46.980 --> 00:25:50.640
And it also-- there's an
accessibility argument,

00:25:50.640 --> 00:25:51.390
as well.

00:25:51.390 --> 00:25:54.210
A lot of people
doing AI research

00:25:54.210 --> 00:25:56.020
are operating on
a smaller scale.

00:25:56.020 --> 00:25:59.160
And so if we can make the
small-scale processing better

00:25:59.160 --> 00:26:02.610
and better, then that really
helps accessibility and sort

00:26:02.610 --> 00:26:04.960
of democratizing
this technology.

00:26:04.960 --> 00:26:08.460
And then, for people that
have the resources to use more

00:26:08.460 --> 00:26:11.400
processors to train
a model, then being

00:26:11.400 --> 00:26:15.360
able to have the software scale
to using many GPUs to train

00:26:15.360 --> 00:26:17.970
one model provides really
compelling benefits.

00:26:17.970 --> 00:26:19.770
And so we need that, as well.

00:26:19.770 --> 00:26:22.120
And we focus on both
those things at NVIDIA.

00:26:22.120 --> 00:26:23.180
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:26:23.180 --> 00:26:24.930
You've mentioned a number
of different groups,

00:26:24.930 --> 00:26:26.190
in terms of their
work, and what's

00:26:26.190 --> 00:26:28.080
going on out there in
the deep learning space

00:26:28.080 --> 00:26:29.550
and in machine learning.

00:26:29.550 --> 00:26:32.920
How much are you collaborating
with other outside groups?

00:26:32.920 --> 00:26:37.170
How do you collaborate with
OpenAI, Facebook, Google?

00:26:37.170 --> 00:26:38.670
BRYAN CATANZARO:
NVIDIA collaborates

00:26:38.670 --> 00:26:41.960
with pretty much everyone.

00:26:41.960 --> 00:26:44.490
I think we work pretty
hard on our relationships

00:26:44.490 --> 00:26:49.590
with research labs, and academic
labs, and industrial labs.

00:26:49.590 --> 00:26:53.850
The way we collaborate,
we like to basically help

00:26:53.850 --> 00:26:54.970
people solve problems.

00:26:54.970 --> 00:26:57.930
So people often
come to us and say,

00:26:57.930 --> 00:26:59.970
we're having problems
getting good efficiency

00:26:59.970 --> 00:27:01.360
with this particular model.

00:27:01.360 --> 00:27:02.640
Can you help us?

00:27:02.640 --> 00:27:04.380
And we'll look into
it and realize, oh,

00:27:04.380 --> 00:27:08.250
cuDNN is choosing the
wrong implementation

00:27:08.250 --> 00:27:11.470
for this particular convolution,
in this particular case.

00:27:11.470 --> 00:27:15.060
So we can change the heuristic
and make that more efficient.

00:27:15.060 --> 00:27:18.390
Or we can maybe add
a new implementation

00:27:18.390 --> 00:27:20.430
for one of those
primitives that turns out

00:27:20.430 --> 00:27:23.190
can be a lot better fitted
to the architecture.

00:27:23.190 --> 00:27:26.430
And so we're constantly
iterating with researchers

00:27:26.430 --> 00:27:29.460
from all over the world
about what their needs are

00:27:29.460 --> 00:27:31.671
and how we can make
our products better.

00:27:31.671 --> 00:27:34.170
MELANIE WARRICK: What are you
most excited about in terms of

00:27:34.170 --> 00:27:36.979
where things are going with
AI and machine learning?

00:27:36.979 --> 00:27:39.270
BRYAN CATANZARO: I'm really
excited about applications.

00:27:39.270 --> 00:27:42.010
That's what I'm
doing for a living.

00:27:42.010 --> 00:27:45.960
I feel like there are just
so many new ways of applying

00:27:45.960 --> 00:27:49.980
this technology to pretty much
every field inside the world

00:27:49.980 --> 00:27:51.345
economy.

00:27:51.345 --> 00:27:53.235
We talk a lot about
self-driving cars.

00:27:53.235 --> 00:27:55.110
And I'm very excited
about self-driving cars.

00:27:55.110 --> 00:27:58.020
I think self-driving cars are
one of the applications that

00:27:58.020 --> 00:28:01.080
are kind of on the vanguard
of really changing the world.

00:28:01.080 --> 00:28:02.700
I'm really excited
about never having

00:28:02.700 --> 00:28:04.230
to find a parking spot again.

00:28:04.230 --> 00:28:07.830
And building houses where
all those parking lots are,

00:28:07.830 --> 00:28:09.870
that would be really great
here in the Bay Area,

00:28:09.870 --> 00:28:11.677
where there's not enough space.

00:28:11.677 --> 00:28:14.010
So I think everybody is excited
about self-driving cars,

00:28:14.010 --> 00:28:16.260
but I think that's
just the beginning.

00:28:16.260 --> 00:28:20.760
I think every industry, from
agriculture to education

00:28:20.760 --> 00:28:25.451
to art, there's just going to
be an explosion of applications

00:28:25.451 --> 00:28:25.950
of AI.

00:28:25.950 --> 00:28:27.576
And it's exciting to
be a part of that.

00:28:27.576 --> 00:28:29.700
MELANIE WARRICK: And for
those who are starting out

00:28:29.700 --> 00:28:32.490
wanting to get into, I mean,
even just doing chip design,

00:28:32.490 --> 00:28:37.680
or trying to get into parallel
processing and deep learning,

00:28:37.680 --> 00:28:41.040
do you have any recommendations,
or any things that you'd say

00:28:41.040 --> 00:28:43.770
spend some time working
on, or some resources that

00:28:43.770 --> 00:28:44.980
come to mind?

00:28:44.980 --> 00:28:46.080
BRYAN CATANZARO: Yeah.

00:28:46.080 --> 00:28:46.500
Well, let's see.

00:28:46.500 --> 00:28:48.499
There's a couple of things
on my mind right now.

00:28:48.499 --> 00:28:52.140
One of them-- so, I saw Google
put out a class recently,

00:28:52.140 --> 00:28:54.000
sort of an introduction
to machine learning.

00:28:54.000 --> 00:28:55.250
I looked through that briefly.

00:28:55.250 --> 00:28:56.240
It looked pretty good.

00:28:56.240 --> 00:28:59.200
So that seems like an
interesting thing to look at.

00:28:59.200 --> 00:29:02.160
NVIDIA also has something called
the Deep Learning Institute,

00:29:02.160 --> 00:29:05.940
where we give tutorials to help
people get hands-on experience

00:29:05.940 --> 00:29:09.450
training models and using
deep learning frameworks.

00:29:09.450 --> 00:29:12.540
I think that has been pretty
interesting for some people.

00:29:12.540 --> 00:29:17.100
I'm also a fan of some of these
online education programs.

00:29:17.100 --> 00:29:19.170
For example, I
work with Udacity.

00:29:19.170 --> 00:29:23.130
Udacity has some great
nanodegrees in AI

00:29:23.130 --> 00:29:25.140
that I think people
can get a lot of value

00:29:25.140 --> 00:29:26.520
from going through.

00:29:26.520 --> 00:29:29.670
And then, I guess, the two other
things that also come to mind

00:29:29.670 --> 00:29:34.530
are open source-- so, there's
just so much out there.

00:29:34.530 --> 00:29:37.530
If you search on the
open source, on GitHub,

00:29:37.530 --> 00:29:40.620
you can find implementations
of all sorts of really

00:29:40.620 --> 00:29:41.970
amazing deep learning models.

00:29:41.970 --> 00:29:43.530
And you can download
them for free

00:29:43.530 --> 00:29:45.820
and just start playing
around with them.

00:29:45.820 --> 00:29:48.570
You don't even need to build
a machine that has a GP unit.

00:29:48.570 --> 00:29:51.550
You can use a cloud service,
like the Google Cloud.

00:29:51.550 --> 00:29:52.300
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:29:52.300 --> 00:29:52.500
Nice.

00:29:52.500 --> 00:29:52.840
Thank you.

00:29:52.840 --> 00:29:55.048
BRYAN CATANZARO: And you
can get started really easy.

00:29:55.048 --> 00:29:57.906
You don't have to invest
a whole bunch to jump in.

00:29:57.906 --> 00:29:59.280
And then, I guess,
the last thing

00:29:59.280 --> 00:30:00.957
that I wanted to mention, also--

00:30:00.957 --> 00:30:02.790
I feel like I'm advertising
for Google here,

00:30:02.790 --> 00:30:05.730
but it is actually a really
good thing-- is Kaggle.

00:30:05.730 --> 00:30:08.940
So Kaggle is this
online competition,

00:30:08.940 --> 00:30:12.900
where people can try to
solve real problems using AI.

00:30:12.900 --> 00:30:17.750
And as somebody that's always
looking for talent to come

00:30:17.750 --> 00:30:20.750
help us apply AI
to new problems,

00:30:20.750 --> 00:30:22.700
when I see a resume
with somebody that's

00:30:22.700 --> 00:30:26.370
been competitive in Kaggle,
I feel like, wow, this

00:30:26.370 --> 00:30:30.710
is a person who is excited about
jumping in to solve problems

00:30:30.710 --> 00:30:32.270
and is very hands-on.

00:30:32.270 --> 00:30:33.770
And I think Kaggle
