00:30:33.770 --> 00:30:36.666
to learn practical
skills about applying AI.

00:30:36.666 --> 00:30:37.540
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:30:37.540 --> 00:30:39.539
Also, one question before
we end up wrapping up.

00:30:39.539 --> 00:30:41.300
As someone who's been
involved, especially

00:30:41.300 --> 00:30:44.680
on the computational side of
things, for such a long period

00:30:44.680 --> 00:30:47.295
and seen the history, I'm
curious to hear about what's

00:30:47.295 --> 00:30:48.920
been the most surprising
thing for you,

00:30:48.920 --> 00:30:50.336
or the most
surprising application

00:30:50.336 --> 00:30:52.580
you've seen come out of
this stuff, or maybe even

00:30:52.580 --> 00:30:54.530
the most interesting,
or weird, or wacky.

00:30:54.530 --> 00:30:57.680
BRYAN CATANZARO: Well, one of
the most surprising, and weird,

00:30:57.680 --> 00:30:59.930
and wacky papers
that I saw recently

00:30:59.930 --> 00:31:03.020
was this one called
the "Deep Image Prior."

00:31:03.020 --> 00:31:04.820
Basically what they're
doing is, instead

00:31:04.820 --> 00:31:08.420
of training a deep learning
model on like a million images,

00:31:08.420 --> 00:31:11.850
they train a deep learning
model on one image.

00:31:11.850 --> 00:31:13.400
And this is a super crazy idea.

00:31:13.400 --> 00:31:16.130
But the results are
actually kind of compelling,

00:31:16.130 --> 00:31:19.220
that they're able to do some
tasks like image completion.

00:31:19.220 --> 00:31:22.550
Imagine that you have a part
of the image that's damaged,

00:31:22.550 --> 00:31:23.900
and you want to repair it.

00:31:23.900 --> 00:31:25.550
They're able to
fill in that image

00:31:25.550 --> 00:31:27.230
by training a deep
learning model just

00:31:27.230 --> 00:31:30.080
on that very single
image, all by itself.

00:31:30.080 --> 00:31:33.140
I thought that was pretty crazy.

00:31:33.140 --> 00:31:36.860
I think there's a ton of
interesting applications

00:31:36.860 --> 00:31:38.810
in agriculture.

00:31:38.810 --> 00:31:41.720
I remember this blog post
from Google, actually,

00:31:41.720 --> 00:31:43.450
about the cucumber sorting.

00:31:43.450 --> 00:31:44.700
Do you guys remember that?

00:31:44.700 --> 00:31:45.290
I think that was really--

00:31:45.290 --> 00:31:46.670
MELANIE WARRICK: One of
our colleagues put that one

00:31:46.670 --> 00:31:47.535
together, actually.

00:31:47.535 --> 00:31:49.160
BRYAN CATANZARO: I
love that blog post.

00:31:49.160 --> 00:31:50.300
I always reference it.

00:31:50.300 --> 00:31:52.820
Because it was very
surprising to me that--

00:31:52.820 --> 00:31:54.590
I don't know too many
cucumber farmers.

00:31:54.590 --> 00:31:57.170
So my image of them is
maybe a little wrong.

00:31:57.170 --> 00:31:59.810
But I just wouldn't
imagine a cucumber farmer

00:31:59.810 --> 00:32:01.790
picking up TensorFlow
and training a model

00:32:01.790 --> 00:32:04.910
to solve a problem that
they had on their farm.

00:32:04.910 --> 00:32:06.370
And I thought that
was really cool.

00:32:06.370 --> 00:32:06.750
MELANIE WARRICK: Agreed.

00:32:06.750 --> 00:32:08.210
That was definitely a fun one.

00:32:08.210 --> 00:32:09.710
Well, as Mark was
mentioning, I know

00:32:09.710 --> 00:32:10.790
we're getting short on time.

00:32:10.790 --> 00:32:12.290
But was there
anything else that you

00:32:12.290 --> 00:32:15.514
wanted to touch on or
cover before we let you go?

00:32:15.514 --> 00:32:16.430
BRYAN CATANZARO: Yeah.

00:32:16.430 --> 00:32:18.890
I guess one of the
things that I think

00:32:18.890 --> 00:32:22.580
is really important in
the advancement of AI

00:32:22.580 --> 00:32:24.530
is the software environment.

00:32:24.530 --> 00:32:25.790
Maybe that's obvious to say.

00:32:25.790 --> 00:32:29.010
But we still have a lot of
work to do to make it better.

00:32:29.010 --> 00:32:31.520
And the environments
that we have now

00:32:31.520 --> 00:32:34.160
represent a huge
amount of investment.

00:32:34.160 --> 00:32:36.930
And I think sometimes we
take them for granted.

00:32:36.930 --> 00:32:39.380
But I just wanted
to sort of highlight

00:32:39.380 --> 00:32:42.140
that, as we progress
forward, there's

00:32:42.140 --> 00:32:46.070
a lot of different ways of
increasing compute capacity

00:32:46.070 --> 00:32:48.670
for deep learning, and for AI.

00:32:48.670 --> 00:32:52.070
And I think that it's
important for all of us

00:32:52.070 --> 00:32:55.040
to remember that it
has to sort of connect

00:32:55.040 --> 00:32:57.665
all the way to the developers.

00:32:57.665 --> 00:32:59.790
And the way that happens
is through the frameworks.

00:32:59.790 --> 00:33:02.600
And so I wanted to kind of give
a shout-out to all the people

00:33:02.600 --> 00:33:05.930
that are building TensorFlow,
and PyTorch, and all

00:33:05.930 --> 00:33:08.419
the frameworks that people
use to train and deploy

00:33:08.419 --> 00:33:08.960
these models.

00:33:08.960 --> 00:33:12.470
Because I think that software
stack is incredibly important.

00:33:12.470 --> 00:33:13.950
And it's changing rapidly.

00:33:13.950 --> 00:33:17.120
And I think we're going to see
a lot of advancements there

00:33:17.120 --> 00:33:19.100
that make deep
learning available

00:33:19.100 --> 00:33:20.550
for even more applications.

00:33:20.550 --> 00:33:21.590
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:33:21.590 --> 00:33:23.430
Well, Bryan, thank you
again for joining us.

00:33:23.430 --> 00:33:24.431
We really appreciate it.

00:33:24.431 --> 00:33:25.388
BRYAN CATANZARO: Great.

00:33:25.388 --> 00:33:26.090
It's been fun.

00:33:26.090 --> 00:33:27.290
MARK MANDEL: Thanks.

00:33:27.290 --> 00:33:27.620
MELANIE WARRICK: All right.

00:33:27.620 --> 00:33:29.180
Well, thank you again, Bryan.

00:33:29.180 --> 00:33:31.460
That was great to talk
to you about what's

00:33:31.460 --> 00:33:33.350
going on at NVIDIA, and
what kind of research

00:33:33.350 --> 00:33:34.850
your group is doing.

00:33:34.850 --> 00:33:36.560
And as we mentioned
in the cool things,

00:33:36.560 --> 00:33:39.950
we have the NVIDIA Tesla
V100s that are the world's

00:33:39.950 --> 00:33:41.810
most advanced data center GPU.

00:33:41.810 --> 00:33:44.450
That's going to be available
in the Google Cloud.

00:33:44.450 --> 00:33:47.900
Now I think it's time for us to
dive into question of the week.

00:33:47.900 --> 00:33:50.240
And so the question of
the week roughly is--

00:33:50.240 --> 00:33:54.260
Sherol, Mark, you both
have been to GDC a lot.

00:33:54.260 --> 00:33:57.560
And you know a lot about the
conference and the community.

00:33:57.560 --> 00:33:59.957
So what can you tell us
as experts of the space?

00:33:59.957 --> 00:34:01.790
MARK MANDEL: I think
Sherol should go first.

00:34:01.790 --> 00:34:04.085
She's been going for
way longer than I have.

00:34:04.085 --> 00:34:04.699
[LAUGHS]

00:34:04.699 --> 00:34:06.740
SHEROL CHEN: I think at
some point in my computer

00:34:06.740 --> 00:34:10.340
science undergrad, I was
like, the reason I'm in this

00:34:10.340 --> 00:34:13.300
was to make video
games, ever since I was

00:34:13.300 --> 00:34:15.110
five years old or something.

00:34:15.110 --> 00:34:17.360
And I was like, where
do I go to find out?

00:34:17.360 --> 00:34:21.139
So then I went to
my first GDC and was

00:34:21.139 --> 00:34:25.159
amazed, because back then,
when I was an undergrad,

00:34:25.159 --> 00:34:29.290
people were like, making games,
you can't do that for a living.

00:34:29.290 --> 00:34:32.719
And then I found this huge event
with like tens of thousands

00:34:32.719 --> 00:34:35.150
of people, where that's
what everybody did.

00:34:35.150 --> 00:34:37.654
And I have been going
for 10 years to GDC.

00:34:37.654 --> 00:34:38.570
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:34:38.570 --> 00:34:40.429
And how long have you
been going for, Mark?

00:34:40.429 --> 00:34:42.137
MARK MANDEL: Like two
years, three years.

00:34:42.137 --> 00:34:42.949
[LAUGHTER]

00:34:43.449 --> 00:34:45.949
I feel like I'm still taking
baby steps, compared to Sherol.

00:34:45.949 --> 00:34:47.990
MELANIE WARRICK: Well,
what's your favorite thing

00:34:47.990 --> 00:34:48.801
about going to GDC?

00:34:48.801 --> 00:34:51.050
SHEROL CHEN: I think this
is probably similar to Mark.

00:34:51.050 --> 00:34:52.130
We both love games.

00:34:52.130 --> 00:34:53.420
We love playing games.

00:34:53.420 --> 00:34:57.380
We like the idea of that
creative space of making games.

00:34:57.380 --> 00:35:00.230
We love new experiences
that come through games.

00:35:00.230 --> 00:35:02.480
And GDC is where
you kind of find

00:35:02.480 --> 00:35:05.000
the people that
are like us, people

00:35:05.000 --> 00:35:09.020
who are out there creating
and innovating in this space.

00:35:09.020 --> 00:35:12.500
And after a few
years, it just becomes

00:35:12.500 --> 00:35:14.800
about connecting
with developers.

00:35:14.800 --> 00:35:15.550
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:35:15.550 --> 00:35:17.591
I would I would echo that
100%, what Sherol said.

00:35:17.591 --> 00:35:19.270
Definitely, it's the people.

00:35:19.270 --> 00:35:21.730
The diversity of people and
the backgrounds that they have

00:35:21.730 --> 00:35:22.610
is wonderful.

00:35:22.610 --> 00:35:25.210
I meet so many people who
come from academic backgrounds

00:35:25.210 --> 00:35:27.022
with a variety of
research, people

00:35:27.022 --> 00:35:28.480
who come from
creative backgrounds,

00:35:28.480 --> 00:35:31.180
from writing backgrounds,
from UX, from programming.

00:35:31.180 --> 00:35:34.930
It is always a interesting
mix of ideas and people

00:35:34.930 --> 00:35:38.437
coming together to produce some
often very interesting things.

00:35:38.437 --> 00:35:41.020
MELANIE WARRICK: Granted, we're
releasing this on a Wednesday,

00:35:41.020 --> 00:35:41.540
as always.

00:35:41.540 --> 00:35:43.000
It's in the middle of
the week, in the middle

00:35:43.000 --> 00:35:44.050
of the conference.

00:35:44.050 --> 00:35:46.750
But what is some of the
activities that you're like,

00:35:46.750 --> 00:35:48.970
you must go to this,
or you must check this

00:35:48.970 --> 00:35:51.700
out when you go to GDC,
especially if you're

00:35:51.700 --> 00:35:53.470
new to GDC?

00:35:53.470 --> 00:35:56.590
SHEROL CHEN: So, for me, I
think one of the greatest

00:35:56.590 --> 00:35:59.200
gateways to GDC is the parties.

00:35:59.200 --> 00:36:01.630
It's great because--
after going once,

00:36:01.630 --> 00:36:03.820
